# Executive Summary  
## Pennsylvania ACA Individual and Small Group Market, Plan Year 2027

### Scope

The audit package examines Pennsylvania’s individual and small group health insurance markets for Plan Year 2027 (PY 2027). Its scope includes geographic rating areas, single risk pool and rate review requirements, plan crosswalk and re-enrollment procedures, benefit design and cost-sharing classifications, and Pennie marketplace operations.

### Reported Market Findings

The package reports a requested average gross rate increase of approximately 16.0% for PY 2027, following a reported average increase of 21.5% for PY 2026. It also reports that enrollment declined from 501,459 to 417,158 by September 2026—a decrease of 84,301 enrollees, or approximately 16.8% of the initial total.

These figures are attributed to an internal audit package and have not been independently verified here. Before publication, they should be checked against the underlying Pennsylvania Insurance Department filings and Pennie enrollment records.

### Calculations and Trend Estimates

The package presents calculations for enrollment change, projected medical cost trends, and other market estimates. The compounded medical trend calculations yield approximately 8.71% at the low end and 12.44% at the high end. These projections should not be described as reconciling the separate historical trend range of 7.4%–9.1% unless the underlying data establish that the figures cover comparable periods and components.

### Statutory Citation

The correction log’s proposed change from ACA § 1343 to § 1340 should be revisited. ACA § 1343 establishes the risk adjustment program and is codified at 42 U.S.C. § 18063; the table should cite § 1343. <citation src="2,3"></citation>

### Verification Caveat

The package’s legal citations, rate figures, enrollment totals, demographic statistics, and operational findings should be validated against their underlying sources before external distribution. Claims based on confidential actuarial exhibits or internal enrollment data should be identified accordingly, and estimates should be distinguished from independently verified results.
